133
00:09:20,419 --> 00:09:26,004
Stage one is Torch export exporter
using PyTorch to graph capture.

134
00:09:26,462 --> 00:09:30,303
This traces our model and produce
a clean and static computation

135
00:09:30,307 --> 00:09:32,817
graph in the core ATEN dialect.

136
00:09:33,441 --> 00:09:37,029
Every ten of Python's overhead
disappears right here.

137
00:09:37,714 --> 00:09:39,038
Stage two is to Edge.

138
00:09:39,944 --> 00:09:44,369
This lowers the ATEN graph into an ExecuTorch Edge dialect, normalizing your

139
00:09:44,369 --> 00:09:49,263
operators looking in float thirty-two
tensor shops, and preparing the

140
00:09:49,267 --> 00:09:51,481
graph for target hardware dialects.

141
00:09:52,671 --> 00:09:54,878
Stage three is to ExecuTorch.

142
00:09:55,996 --> 00:10:00,606
Here, the compiler performs static
memory planning, pre-calculating memory,

143
00:10:00,770 --> 00:10:04,659
array know offsets of intermediate
tensor at compile time, so the Android

144
00:10:04,659 --> 00:10:09,143
phone never have to slow down dynamic
memory, locations during inferences.

145
00:10:09,636 --> 00:10:14,813
Finally, we write ET program buffer
directly into a readiness forecaster PTE

146
00:10:15,813 --> 00:10:21,621
Having a PDE file is great, but
shipping into a native C++ runtime

147
00:10:21,621 --> 00:10:25,939
